@@ -69,6 +69,24 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a JSON document supplied as text, with syntax highlighting. The output is valid JSON, indented by 4 spaces by default. Pass ``indent`` to change the number of spaces, and ``highlight=False`` to disable highlighting::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The JSON is rendered by the :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable, which you can also pass to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`. The renderable disables word wrapping, but under default printing lines that are wider than the console are cropped to the console width. To keep long lines whole, enable soft wrapping by setting ``soft_wrap=True`` when printing.
+
+There is also a package level function, which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "rich"}')
+
+From the command line, run the json module with the path to a JSON file to pretty print it::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
